+## A two-tone ink post pass (labyrinth-larry `ink.js`)
+
+Use it to give a lit three.js game a second, graphic art style without
+touching meshes. It's the house style *Sin City ink* (docs/06 §7).
+
+1. **Encode roles in the colour buffer.** Role-patched materials write grey
+   (`mono`), white (`paper`) or **key-red** `vec3(brightness, 0, 0)` (`accent`).
+   The post pass finds accents with `key = r − max(g, b) > 0.08`. Additive
+   accent sprites still work: they add to red only. Coloured lights don't
+   matter, because the patch greys the *lit* result.
+2. **Render to a target with a depth texture:**
+   `new WebGLRenderTarget(w·dpr, h·dpr, { depthTexture: new DepthTexture() })`.
+   Resize it with the canvas.
+3. **One full-screen quad** (`PlaneGeometry(2,2)`, a vertex shader that writes
+   `position.xy` straight to clip space, `depthTest/depthWrite: false`):
+   - Perceptual luminance `pow(lum, 1/2.2)`, since the render target is linear.
+   - Step to ink / hatch / paper: thresholds 0.21, 0.30, 0.38, 0.46.
+   - Hatch screens use `gl_FragCoord.xy / dpr`, so line spacing is in CSS px.
+   - Depth edges: `max(|d(x+1) − d(x−1)|, |d(y+1) − d(y−1)|) > 0.0035`
+     (ortho, near 1 / far 200, so about 0.7 world units). Draw them in the
+     tone opposite the pixel.
+   - `depth == 1` is the void: ink plus hashed falling streaks.
+   - End with `#include <colorspace_fragment>` so palette colours set via
+     `THREE.Color` come out right.
+4. **Cost:** one extra full-screen pass, only in ink mode. The classic path
+   calls `renderer.render` directly and pays nothing.
+5. **Author for it:**
+   - Tag hot things `markAccent(obj)`, the hero `markRole(obj, 'paper')`
+     (pre-tag eyes and mouth `'mono'` first; roles don't overwrite), and glows
+     `inkHide(obj)`.
+   - Call `inkify(scene)` and `applyInkVisibility(scene)` after every level
+     build.
+
